Good faith is the principle that participation in an agreement, relationship, negotiation, or system should be genuine rather than merely technically compliant.

At its core:

Good faith = acting consistently with the legitimate purpose and shared understanding of an arrangement, not exploiting its wording to defeat that purpose.

That distinction matters. There are at least three layers:

1. Literal compliance — “Did I obey the words?”
2. Good-faith compliance — “Did I honor what those words were genuinely meant to accomplish?”
3. Bad-faith compliance — “Can I obey the words while deliberately frustrating their purpose?”

That maps directly onto what you were just saying about the Star Act. If the understood substantive objective is remove the offset so eligible veterans can receive both earned retired pay and VA disability compensation, legislation could theoretically preserve the name or claim to provide “concurrent receipt” while introducing a different formula, cap, offset, or funding mechanism that substantially recreates the original loss. The relevant analytical question then isn’t merely “Did they pass something called the Major Richard Star Act?” but “Did the enacted mechanism faithfully instantiate the benefit Congress and its supporters represented it as providing?”

There’s an even broader Pantheon primitive here:

Good faith is semantic fidelity under freedom of interpretation.

The more discretion an actor has over how to satisfy a commitment, the more important good faith becomes. You can’t specify every possible exploit in advance. Good faith fills the space between what can be formally encoded and what the parties actually meant.

That makes its inverse useful too:

Bad faith = exploiting the delta between representation and intent.

And that delta is measurable. Compare stated purpose → operative mechanism → actual outcome. Persistent divergence, particularly when advantageous to the actor controlling the mechanism, is evidence worth investigating rather than simply accepting the label attached to it.